    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_acab5f3235f3da95a32f4fd15f1b8111b2ed06c833e4f019d5961c8d62deacf8",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Two different saddle constructions measure different sides of the linear-form method. The frequency decomposition and its admissible contour deformations determine the exponential magnitude rate κ of I_n. Separate horizontal auxiliary integrals, governed by an imaginary root, sharpen the exponential bound for the coefficients A_s. In the final arithmetic argument the first rate contributes to the potential usefulness of the normalized linear form, whereas the second controls the coefficient cost relevant to independence estimates.",
